text:
O Love! I know that thou art wise,

 And wilt be always so;

 But how dost thou rule thy own desire?
 Thou must not have it more than me;

In the garden where flowers bloom red or blue,
I saw butterflies on wings so light as feather;
They were coming like little birds to me;
And they came down low with feet so small and clear.

The rose was white and pink and purple too;
Then yellow lilies stood up very tall,
Like golden spears that waved above my head,
And the daisies smiled at me through their petals.

Then he turns to some hill which is high;
There’s a tower where the knights always fly :
The towers so fair are not on any sea,
But on top of mountaintops they do flee ;
They look down from their lofty height at thee;

The moon was there, and all her light did fall;
 And yet she seemed to take no thought for us, but only waited till we should be gone;
 Then she arose with a little sigh from heaven’s gate,
And came through our door at nightfall that day;

“Forth from that green spot, which now is not,
And more than little, and less than large,—

The white-haired woman with the gray skirts spread ;
